= October 1949

Reports of interference in the elections to the Sixteenth
{!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!} have brought
{!<span class="term term-mossadegh">!}Mohammad Mossadegh{!</span>!} and other
opposition politicians together. Individual candidates can protest their own
returns; acting together would turn those complaints into a public demand for
representative elections.

The group goes to the royal palace to press that demand. It is not yet a
disciplined party, and the protest does not give it control over the election.
What it creates is a shared political purpose and a place where politicians
with different organizations and constituencies can continue cooperating.

[? if election_preparation_response = "Election-monitoring committee": The
monitoring committee arrives with comparable reports, but no authority to
compel a remedy. ?]
[? if election_preparation_response = "Public constitutional appeal": The
public appeal has already named the election as a constitutional question,
raising the cost of a quiet settlement. ?]
[? if election_preparation_response = "Provincial correspondents": The
provincial correspondence supplies a wider record while leaving the opposition
without one public voice. ?]

- @begin_organizing

@begin_organizing
title: Carry the protest into an organized election campaign
subtitle: The palace protest becomes the place where separate election complaints begin to speak together

The protesters leave without a final settlement of the disputed returns.
They do, however, continue meeting, comparing election complaints, and
defining the constitutional grounds on which they can act together.

The new cooperation still contains separate loyalties, unequal organizations,
and different ideas about how far the alliance should extend.

- @post_event: Continue
